+# RSV Prevention Brief
+
+## Scope
+
+This U.S. educational brief is for a 69-year-old in the continental United States who has chronic lung disease and has never received an RSV vaccine. It summarizes prevention guidance current as of August 15, 2026. It is not individualized medical advice; a healthcare professional can assess medical history, precautions, and local timing.
+
+## Sourced facts
+
+CDC recommends one single dose of an RSV vaccine for adults ages 50–74 who are at increased risk of severe RSV illness. Chronic lung or respiratory disease is specifically listed as a risk factor, so this profile falls within that risk-based recommendation. CDC lists three vaccines for adults 50 and older and expresses no preference among them ([CDC, “RSV Vaccine Guidance for Adults”](https://www.cdc.gov/rsv/hcp/vaccine-clinical-guidance/adults.html)).
+
+RSV vaccination is not currently an annual vaccination. Someone who already received a dose—including in a prior season—has completed the currently recommended vaccination and should not receive another dose at this time. CDC notes that evidence about whether additional doses may be useful is still being evaluated, so this could change after the brief’s access date ([CDC adult guidance](https://www.cdc.gov/rsv/hcp/vaccine-clinical-guidance/adults.html)).
+
+An eligible, previously unvaccinated adult can be vaccinated at any time of year. In most of the continental United States, the preferred timing is late summer or early fall—August–October—before RSV usually begins spreading. Vaccination can occur during the same visit as other adult vaccines, though side effects may be more noticeable with coadministration ([CDC adult guidance](https://www.cdc.gov/rsv/hcp/vaccine-clinical-guidance/adults.html)).
+
+## Options comparison
+
+All three products are active vaccines intended to prevent RSV lower respiratory tract disease. The FDA pages establish each product’s indication for this age, while CDC’s recommendation determines how vaccination fits this risk profile. Effectiveness figures from separate studies should not be treated as direct head-to-head comparisons.
+
+| Product | Platform / manufacturer | Fit for this 69-year-old | Practical distinction |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| [Arexvy](https://www.fda.gov/vaccines-blood-biologics/arexvy) | Adjuvanted protein vaccine; GSK | FDA-indicated from age 60; one of the CDC options | Reconstituted before injection; the adjuvant is a product-level difference. |
+| [Abrysvo](https://www.fda.gov/vaccines-blood-biologics/abrysvo) | Bivalent protein vaccine; Pfizer | FDA-indicated from age 60; one of the CDC options | Non-adjuvanted; also has other indications that do not alter this adult decision. |
+| [mResvia](https://www.fda.gov/vaccines-blood-biologics/vaccines/mresvia) | mRNA vaccine; Moderna | FDA-indicated from age 60; one of the CDC options | Supplied as a pre-filled syringe and uses an mRNA platform. |
+
+CDC does not prefer one of these vaccines. Availability, allergies or prior reactions, administration considerations, and a clinician’s review can guide product choice without changing the one-dose recommendation ([CDC adult guidance](https://www.cdc.gov/rsv/hcp/vaccine-clinical-guidance/adults.html)).
+
+## Questions for a clinician
+
+- Can we confirm that no RSV vaccine was received previously, including under a brand name?
+- Does the specific chronic lung condition or its current severity affect vaccination timing or precautions?
+- Is there any allergy, prior vaccine reaction, or current illness that changes which product or visit date is appropriate?
+- Which of the three products is available locally, and is there a practical reason to choose one for this person?
+- Should the RSV vaccine be given with other due vaccines or at a separate visit to manage expected side effects?
+
